@meta
id: 07-07
title: Count the blocks, not the lines
chapter: 07
type: lesson
skills: paragraph-motion, find-f
par: 46
minutes: 6
vscode: scroll and count test blocks on the screen
open: order.provider.spec.ts

@brief
Fourteen tests, 100 lines, one blank line between each pair. Three of them
have gone flaky against the new payment sandbox and are being skipped until
ORD-418 lands: the fourth, the ninth and the fourteenth.

Turn `it(` into `it.skip(` on those three, and only those three.

You already know how to step over one block at a time. The blocks here are
evenly spaced and you know which numbers you want, so stepping five times
and stepping once should cost the same.

@keys
| `5}` | forward five paragraphs |
| `2{` | back two paragraphs |
| `f(` | forward to the open paren |
| `i` | insert before the cursor |

@hints
Every motion you have met takes a count, and the paragraph motions are no
exception. Work out how many gaps lie between you and the block you want.
--
`5}` moves forward five blank lines in one go. From the top of the file the
gaps are: after the imports, then one before each test. The fourth test
therefore sits one line below the fifth gap.
--
The rep is `5}` `j` to get onto the `it(` line, then `f(` `i` `.skip` `Esc`
to write the word in front of the paren. Each of the next two blocks is
another five gaps away, so the same `5}` `j` repeats exactly.

@solution
    5} j f ( i .skip <Esc>
    5} j f ( i .skip <Esc>
    5} j f ( i .skip <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Forty keystrokes, and the same twelve three times.

The count is not an optimisation, it is a change of unit. `5}` says "five
blocks down" and lands on the right one whether each block is four lines or
forty. `40j` says "forty lines down" and is wrong the moment somebody adds
an assertion. Every counted motion you have learned behaves this way —
`5j` counts lines, `5w` counts words, `5}` counts blocks — and the skill is
picking the unit that matches what you are looking at. Here you were handed
ordinals, so blocks was the unit.

`{` takes a count too. `2{` is up two blocks, and it is the motion you want
when a test fails and you need the `beforeEach` above it.

One habit to carry forward: when the same short sequence repeats three
times, notice it. In chapter 11 the dot command will replay the last change
for one keystroke, and in chapter 21 you will record the whole rep once and
run it fourteen times. Both of those only pay off if you are writing your
edits as a repeatable rep in the first place, which is exactly what `5}` `j`
`f(` `i.skip` `Esc` is.

@start order.provider.spec.ts
import { stub, reveal } from 'jest-auto-stub';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { orderFactory, cancelOrderArgsFactory } from '../../../test/factories';

describe('OrderProvider', () => {
  const orderRepository = stub<OrderRepository>();
  const paymentService = stub<PaymentService>();
  const provider = new OrderProvider(orderRepository, paymentService);
  afterEach(() => {
    jest.resetAllMocks();
  });

  it('returns the order when one exists', async () => {
    const order = orderFactory();
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(order);
    expect(await provider.findOne(order.id)).toEqual(order);
  });

  it('throws when the order is missing', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(undefined);
    await expect(provider.findOne('ord_0000')).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
  });

  it('lists orders for a customer, newest first', async () => {
    const orders = [orderFactory(), orderFactory()];
    reveal(orderRepository).findByCustomer.mockResolvedValue(orders);
    expect(await provider.findByCustomer('cus_88')).toEqual(orders);
  });

  it('refunds a cancelled order through the payment service', async () => {
    const order = orderFactory({ status: 'COMPLETED' });
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(order);
    await provider.cancel(cancelOrderArgsFactory({ id: order.id }));
    expect(reveal(paymentService).refund).toHaveBeenCalledWith(order.id);
  });

  it('refuses to cancel an order that has shipped', async () => {
    const order = orderFactory({ status: 'SHIPPED' });
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(order);
    await expect(provider.cancel({ id: order.id })).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
  });

  it('records a cancellation reason', async () => {
    const order = orderFactory({ status: 'PENDING' });
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(order);
    await provider.cancel({ id: order.id, reason: 'CUSTOMER_REQUEST' });
    expect(reveal(orderRepository).update).toHaveBeenCalled();
  });

  it('does not refund outside the thirty day window', async () => {
    const order = orderFactory({ placedAt: new Date('2025-01-01') });
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(order);
    await provider.cancel({ id: order.id });
    expect(reveal(paymentService).refund).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
  });

  it('counts orders by status', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).countByStatus.mockResolvedValue(12);
    expect(await provider.countByStatus('PENDING')).toEqual(12);
  });

  it('creates an order in pending', async () => {
    const order = orderFactory({ status: 'PENDING' });
    reveal(orderRepository).create.mockResolvedValue(order);
    expect(await provider.create({ customerId: 'cus_88', amount: 4999 })).toEqual(order);
  });

  it('defaults the currency to sterling', async () => {
    const order = orderFactory({ currency: 'GBP' });
    reveal(orderRepository).create.mockResolvedValue(order);
    expect((await provider.create({ customerId: 'cus_88', amount: 100 })).currency).toEqual('GBP');
  });

  it('soft deletes rather than removing the row', async () => {
    const order = orderFactory();
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(order);
    await provider.remove(order.id);
    expect(reveal(orderRepository).softDelete).toHaveBeenCalledWith(order.id);
  });

  it('totals completed orders for a customer', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).totalForCustomer.mockResolvedValue(9999);
    expect(await provider.totalForCustomer('cus_88')).toEqual(9999);
  });

  it('reinstates a cancelled order', async () => {
    const order = orderFactory({ status: 'CANCELLED' });
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(order);
    await provider.reinstate(order.id);
    expect(reveal(orderRepository).update).toHaveBeenCalled();
  });

  it('leaves a shipped order alone when reinstating', async () => {
    const order = orderFactory({ status: 'SHIPPED' });
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(order);
    await expect(provider.reinstate(order.id)).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
  });
});

